problem:
Suppose that, for some angles $x$ and $y$,

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \sin ^{2} x+\cos ^{2} y=\frac{3}{2} a \\
& \cos ^{2} x+\sin ^{2} y=\frac{1}{2} a^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Determine the possible value(s) of $a$.

steps:
Constructing a Counterexample: To show that not every \( k \)-jumpy map can be decomposed into \( 1 \)-jumpy maps, we need to find a \( k \)-jumpy map that cannot be achieved by any finite composition of \( 1 \)-jumpy maps. Consider the following \( 2 \)-jumpy map \( f \):
\[ f(z) = \begin{cases}
z + 2 & \text{if } z \text{ is even} \\
z - 2 & \text{if } z \text{ is odd}
\end{cases} \]
This map is clearly \( 2 \)-jumpy since \( |f(z) - z| = 2 \) for all \( z \in \mathbb{Z} \).

Solve the quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \(y = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\), where \(a = 3\), \(b = -7\), and \(c = -6\):
\[
y = \frac{-(-7) \pm \sqrt{(-7)^2 - 4 \cdot 3 \cdot (-6)}}{2 \cdot 3}
\]
\[
y = \frac{7 \pm \sqrt{49 + 72}}{6}
\]
\[
y = \frac{7 \pm \sqrt{121}}{6}
\]
\[
y = \frac{7 \pm 11}{6}
\]

